Chapter: HttpsClient
  cites Net chapter HttpFetch
  cites Encode chapter X509
  cites Encode chapter X509Chain
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Foreword chapter Result

 `fetch-tls` in Net chapter HttpFetch has never met a foreign peer. The
 loopback in `codex/test/apps/tls-fetch-loopback` drives our client with
 our own server over no network at all, and `build/mqtts-interop-test.ps1`
 does meet OpenSSL but re-implements the record framing in
 `tools/mqtts-client.codex` rather than calling this path -- so
 `tls-recv-record`, `https-drive`, `https-request` and `https-recv-app`
 have been exercised only by code that agrees with them.

 That is the shape the compression post-mortem is about, and it is exactly
 how a ClientHello carrying NO signature_algorithms extension survived: our
 own server does not read the extension, so no loopback could see it
 missing. A real server read it and refused.

   4443  an ECDSA P-256 chain. The server's CertificateVerify is
         ecdsa_secp256r1_sha256, and the leaf's own signature is ECDSA
         over SHA-256, so BOTH the new verifier and the new chain arm run
         against bytes OpenSSL produced.
   4444  an RSA-2048 chain. The CertificateVerify is rsa_pss_rsae_sha256
         -- TLS 1.3 permits no substitute for PSS here -- and the
         certificate signatures are PKCS#1 v1.5. Two different RSA
         paddings in one connection.
   4445  the rogue: a genuine, unexpired, correctly named, self-signed
         leaf that no anchor vouches for. It must be REFUSED. Without it
         the two passes above prove only that we accept things, which is
         not the property anybody wants.

 The body is compared byte for byte against a file the harness wrote, so
 a handshake that completes and then delivers the wrong bytes is a
 failure here rather than a success with a footnote.

Section: The Trust Anchors

 The CA certificates, patched in by `build/mint-https-fixtures.ps1 -Patch`
 from the PEMs under `codex/test/fixtures/https/`. The harness re-derives
 them from those PEMs and refuses to run if these have drifted, so there
 is one source of truth and it is checked rather than hoped for.

